WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:04.568
Here is a cart called c1.

2
00:00:04.568 --> 00:00:08.165
Its skus field holds a list with one item, sku-1.

3
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.802
Copy it, and change only the id.

4
00:00:10.802 --> 00:00:13.128
Now there is a second cart, c2.

5
00:00:13.333 --> 00:00:16.292
The copy takes each field's value as it is.

6
00:00:16.292 --> 00:00:18.907
But the value of skus is not the list.

7
00:00:18.907 --> 00:00:20.764
It is an arrow to the list.

8
00:00:20.764 --> 00:00:23.792
So c2 gets a second arrow, to the same list.

9
00:00:23.867 --> 00:00:26.199
Now add sku-2 to c2's list.

10
00:00:26.333 --> 00:00:28.217
What does c1's list hold now?

11
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:30.810
Both items.

12
00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:32.135
Nobody touched c1.

13
00:00:32.135 --> 00:00:36.185
But there is only one list, and both carts point at it.

14
00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:37.917
This is a shallow copy.

15
00:00:37.917 --> 00:00:41.875
It copies the fields, and shares every object they point to.

16
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.456
Kotlin's copy() works this way, and its docs say so.

17
00:00:45.456 --> 00:00:49.245
So does a Java record wither, and so does Object.clone().

18
00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:54.919
So a copy is shallow until you make it deep, one nested object at a time.

19
00:00:54.919 --> 00:00:57.957
The simple way: store a list that can't change.

20
00:00:58.067 --> 00:01:00.527
Now c2 can't add to the shared list.

21
00:01:00.733 --> 00:01:05.896
To change c2's items, you build a new list: the old items plus sku-2.

22
00:01:05.896 --> 00:01:07.467
Only c2 points to it.

23
00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:09.580
c1 still holds one item.

24
00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:11.733
Two lists, and no surprise.

25
00:01:12.867 --> 00:01:17.354
In Kotlin, make the field a read-only List and change it with plus.

26
00:01:17.354 --> 00:01:23.717
In Java, wrap it with List.copyOf in the compact constructor, and let withers build a new list.

27
00:01:24.867 --> 00:01:26.297
Copying is Prototype.

28
00:01:26.297 --> 00:01:29.634
Building an object from scratch has its own trap.

29
00:01:29.634 --> 00:01:30.860
Look at this call.

30
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:32.777
Which value is the timeout?

31
00:01:34.133 --> 00:01:35.339
You have to count.

32
00:01:35.339 --> 00:01:37.684
Nothing at the call site tells you.

33
00:01:37.684 --> 00:01:42.239
The Java fix is a builder: a second class with one method per field.

34
00:01:42.467 --> 00:01:44.432
Kotlin fixes it in the language.

35
00:01:44.432 --> 00:01:49.712
Every argument can carry its parameter's name, and every parameter can have a default.

36
00:01:49.867 --> 00:01:55.313
So the call sets the four values it needs, in any order, and the defaults fill the rest.

37
00:01:55.533 --> 00:01:57.606
copy() gives you a changed copy.

38
00:01:57.606 --> 00:02:02.075
And checks in an init block run on every construction, copy included.

39
00:02:02.267 --> 00:02:04.207
Java has no named arguments.

40
00:02:04.207 --> 00:02:08.296
If Java code calls the class a lot, give it a real builder.

41
00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:10.188
The rule.

42
00:02:10.188 --> 00:02:13.256
A copy shares every object its fields point to.

43
00:02:13.256 --> 00:02:18.217
Keep those objects unchangeable, and build a new one when you need a change.

44
00:02:18.333 --> 00:02:19.338
Quick check.

45
00:02:19.338 --> 00:02:22.772
A Payment holds a MutableMap of metadata.

46
00:02:22.772 --> 00:02:28.048
You make p2 with p1.copy(), then put a retry key into p2's map.

47
00:02:28.048 --> 00:02:29.304
Does p1 see it?

48
00:02:31.667 --> 00:02:31.963
Yes.

49
00:02:31.963 --> 00:02:34.703
copy() copied the arrow, not the map.

50
00:02:34.703 --> 00:02:36.999
Both payments point at one map.
